5:["$","article",null,{"className":"container-site py-14 md:py-20","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/blog","className":"group inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:text-foreground","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-left h-4 w-4 transition-transform duration-300 group-hover:-translate-x-0.5 motion-reduce:transform-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","1l729n",{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7"}],["$","path","x3x0zl",{"d":"M19 12H5"}],"$undefined"]}],"All writing"]}],["$","header",null,{"className":"mt-8 max-w-2xl","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[12px] tracking-wide text-muted-foreground/70","children":[["$","time",null,{"dateTime":"2026-10-08","children":"October 8, 2026"}],["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"h-px w-6 bg-border"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-clock h-3 w-3","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","mmk7yg",{"d":"M12 6v6l4 2"}],["$","circle","1mglay",{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"10"}],"$undefined"]}],6," min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl","children":"What building Telegram bots taught me about software design"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Long-running processes, state that must survive restarts, and interfaces measured in messages — lessons from building Miro, my Python and aiogram bot with memory and music."}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-2","aria-label":"Tags","children":[["$","li","Python",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Python"}],["$","li","aiogram",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"aiogram"}],["$","li","Bots",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Bots"}],["$","li","Software Design",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Software Design"}]]}]]}],["$","$Lf",null,{"className":"mt-12","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-2xl space-y-5","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Most software I build starts, runs, and stops within a request. A Telegram bot does not. It is a process that must stay alive for weeks, remember who it talked to, respond to a dozen people at once, and fail without ever appearing to fail. Building Miro — a Python and aiogram bot with conversational AI, music features, memory and admin commands — taught me more about software design than any course on the subject."}],["$","h2","1",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"The worst failure mode is silence"}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"A web app that crashes shows an error page. A bot that crashes simply stops talking. From the outside, a dead bot and an ignored bot are indistinguishable — the failure is invisible unless you are watching the process. This changes what you optimise for. Uptime is not a metric on a dashboard; it is the bot's entire personality. A bot that vanishes twice a week is not a bot with an availability problem, it is a bot people stop talking to."}],"$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13","$L14","$L15","$L16","$L17","$L18","$L19","$L1a"]}]}],"$L1b"]}]
10:["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"The answer is boring and absolute: every handler gets an error boundary, every background task is supervised, and the process is run under something that restarts it when it dies. None of this is clever. All of it is load-bearing."}]
11:["$","h2","4",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"State is a design decision, not an accident"}]
12:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"A bot accumulates state whether you plan for it or not: which users exist, what they asked for, what the bot was in the middle of doing when a restart happened. My early versions held conversational context in memory, which meant every deploy quietly amputated every ongoing conversation. The lesson was structural: state that matters belongs in storage, and in-memory state is a cache with an attitude problem."}]
13:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Miro now keeps its memory in SQLite, keyed per user and per chat, with retention rules per memory type. It remembers what serves the conversation — names, preferences, running jokes — and deliberately forgets the rest. Memory design is a product decision wearing a database hat."}]
14:["$","pre","7",{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 p-5 font-mono text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-foreground/80","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"what memory teaches you:\n\n  everything remembered = expensive + creepy\n  nothing remembered    = vending machine\n  purposeful memory     = a bot that knows you"}]}]
15:["$","h2","8",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"The message is the interface"}]
16:["$","p","9",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Chat imposes a brutal constraint: your entire interface is text, delivered in the order you send it, with no layout, no hover states and no undo. That constraint is clarifying. There is nowhere to hide a badly designed flow behind a nice screen. If your bot asks for five pieces of information, users feel every one of them."}]
17:["$","p","10",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"The discipline I arrived at: short messages, one idea each, and commands that do one thing. Telegram's formatting is a gift here — a little monospace and a couple of line breaks do more for readability than any amount of words. And an AI-backed bot needs the same restraint: bounded reply lengths, a clear sense of when it is being addressed, and graceful fallback when the AI backend is slow. A bot that posts a paragraph every time it is mentioned is a bot that gets muted."}]
18:["$","h2","11",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"Personality is engineering"}]
19:["$","p","12",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"The temptation with an AI bot is to make it say clever things. The work is making it say things consistently. A personality that drifts between responses feels broken in a way users can name but not diagnose. Consistency of tone, predictable boundaries on length, reliable behaviour when commands collide — these are engineering constraints, and they are what makes a bot feel like a character rather than a random process with a profile picture."}]
1a:["$","p","13",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"I keep Miro experimental and hobby-scale, and I describe it that way, because that is what it is. But the lessons scale far past hobbies: design for the worst failure mode, treat state as architecture, respect the interface's constraints, and be consistent on purpose. Every service I have built since — including this website's contact pipeline — is more reliable for them."}]
